{\b S6-3: Fix ForEach $row binding Undo glitch — both review screens}\par
\par
Files: AITest/Views/SaleEntryReviewView.swift, AITest/Views/PurchaseReviewView.swift\par
\par
Problem: After tapping an item in the SaleItemPickerSheet, the row immediately shows greyed-out with "Undo" (isSkipped appears true). Only after tapping "Undo" does the correctly-linked row appear. This happens in BOTH Review Sales and Review Invoice. Bug persists even after removing pickingItemRowID = nil from the callback.\par
\par
Root cause: ForEach($rows) derives $row bindings using SwiftUI's internal index tracking. When .sheet(item:) dismisses (via dismiss() inside SaleItemPickerSheet) at the same time as rows[i].resolvedItem is mutated, SwiftUI re-renders the ForEach during the dismiss animation. The derived $row binding can read stale state, causing isSkipped to appear true momentarily — and since SwiftUI's state is committed at that point, it stays stuck.\par
\par
Fix: Replace ForEach($rows) with an explicit stable-ID enumerated binding in BOTH files. This bypasses the derived binding entirely.\par
\par
Pattern to use (identical for both files, just with their respective Row/ParsedRow types):\par
\par
// BEFORE in SaleEntryReviewView (rowsList):\par
ForEach($rows) \{ $row in\par
    SaleReviewRow(\par
        row: $row,\par
        allItems: allItems,\par
        currencyManager: currencyManager,\par
        onRequestItemPicker: \{ pickingRowItem = PickingRowID(id: row.id) \}\par
    )\par
\}\par
.onDelete \{ rows.remove(atOffsets: $0) \}\par
\par
// AFTER:\par
ForEach(Array(rows.enumerated()), id: \\.element.id) \{ i, _ in\par
    SaleReviewRow(\par
        row: Binding(\par
            get: \{ i < rows.count ? rows[i] : ParsedSaleRow(itemName: "", quantitySold: 0, pricePerUnit: 0) \},\par
            set: \{ if i < rows.count \{ rows[i] = $0 \} \}\par
        ),\par
        allItems: allItems,\par
        currencyManager: currencyManager,\par
        onRequestItemPicker: \{ if i < rows.count \{ pickingRowItem = PickingRowID(id: rows[i].id) \} \}\par
    )\par
\}\par
.onDelete \{ rows.remove(atOffsets: $0) \}\par
\par
// BEFORE in PurchaseReviewView (rowsList):\par
ForEach($rows) \{ $row in\par
    PurchaseReviewRow(\par
        row: $row,\par
        defaultStorage: defaultStorage,\par
        currencyManager: currencyManager,\par
        onRequestItemPicker: \{ pickingItemRowID = row.id \}\par
    )\par
\}\par
.onDelete \{ rows.remove(atOffsets: $0) \}\par
\par
// AFTER:\par
ForEach(Array(rows.enumerated()), id: \\.element.id) \{ i, _ in\par
    PurchaseReviewRow(\par
        row: Binding(\par
            get: \{ i < rows.count ? rows[i] : ParsedPurchaseRow(itemName: "", quantityReceived: 0, costPerUnit: 0) \},\par
            set: \{ if i < rows.count \{ rows[i] = $0 \} \}\par
        ),\par
        defaultStorage: defaultStorage,\par
        currencyManager: currencyManager,\par
        onRequestItemPicker: \{ if i < rows.count \{ pickingItemRowID = rows[i].id \} \}\par
    )\par
\}\par
.onDelete \{ rows.remove(atOffsets: $0) \}\par
\par
Important: ParsedSaleRow and ParsedPurchaseRow both have let id = UUID() — stable identity. The enumerated id: \\.element.id uses each row's stable UUID so ForEach correctly tracks identity across deletes and re-renders.\par
\par
Also scan: search the entire codebase for other ForEach($array) patterns where a .sheet(item:) is used in the same view to mutate array elements. Apply the same enumerated Binding fix to any found. Likely candidates: VoiceInventoryView, QuickCountView, any other review screens.\par
