FIX (AIInventoryService.swift):\par
1. Add a helper:\par
   private static func todayISO() -> String \{\par
       let df = DateFormatter(); df.locale = Locale(identifier: "en_US_POSIX")\par
       df.timeZone = .current; df.dateFormat = "yyyy-MM-dd"\par
       return df.string(from: Date())\par
   \}\par
2. In parseVoiceTranscript (promptBody), in identifyProduct (photo prompt ~line 185), and in the paper-sheet prompt (~line 334), inject near the top:\par
   "Today's date is \\(Self.todayISO()) (the device's local date)."\par
   and add these Rules lines:\par
   - "Resolve relative expiry expressions ('in 5 days', 'expires next week', 'end of month', 'in 2 months') to an absolute calendar date in YYYY-MM-DD, computed from today's date above."\par
   - "A future expiry MUST be a date AFTER today. Never output a past date unless the user explicitly states the item is already expired."\par
   - "If no expiry is mentioned, return null. Do not invent one."\par
3. Defensive guard in parseExpiryDate(_:): after parsing, if the resulting date is more than 1 day before today AND the source was voice/photo (future-intent), keep it only if the model was explicit; otherwise it's acceptable to still return it (items can be genuinely expired). Do NOT hard-reject -- the real fix is the date anchor. (Leave parseExpiryDate returning the parsed value; the anchor prevents the bad output.)\par
