1. Compute isRTL from the manager: let isRTL = LocalizationManager.shared.layoutDirection == .rightToLeft\par
2. BEFORE creating the UIHostingController, force UIKit semantic direction app-wide so the freshly built tree adopts it:\par
       UIView.appearance().semanticContentAttribute = isRTL ? .forceRightToLeft : .forceLeftToRight\par
3. After building `hosting` and before/at assignment, also set it on the window and hosting view:\par
       window.semanticContentAttribute = isRTL ? .forceRightToLeft : .forceLeftToRight\par
       hosting.view.semanticContentAttribute = isRTL ? .forceRightToLeft : .forceLeftToRight\par
4. Keep the existing .environment(\\.layoutDirection, localizationManager.layoutDirection) (SwiftUI side).\par
5. Also call reRootWindow at first launch if the persisted language is RTL, so a saved Arafbic choice is RTL from cold start. (If not already covered by AppleLanguages at cold start, add a one-time semanticContentAttribute set in app init based on LocalizationManager.shared.layoutDirection.)\par
Note: switching from Arabic BACK to an LTR language must reset to .forceLeftToRight (covered by step 2's ternary).\par
\par
VERIFY (device, live, no relaunch): Settings -> Arabic -> whole UI mirrors (nav back chevron on right, HStacks reversed, text right-aligned, tab bar mirrored). Switch to English -> reverts to LTR. Kill+relaunch in Arabic -> still RTL.\par
